Issues #101 (parseBearer) and #102 (dispatcher stop) already exist — so those two causes are already filed; my groups for those should be marked "duplicate" pointing to the open issue... wait, careful: "duplicate" status means the crash group duplicates an existing open issue. But I still fixed them? The instructions: "file each cause that isn't filed yet" — for causes already filed, I don't file a new issue. For submit_diagnosis, status can be "fixed" (I fixed it, with issue = ...?). Hmm: issue field is "the issue you filed, for status fixed or needs_human". For duplicates, status "duplicate" with duplicate: 101.

But wait — if I fixed the code and there's already an issue, what's the right status? The statuses: fixed, already_fixed, duplicate, not_a_bug, needs_human. "already_fixed" presumably means the code is already fixed (no action needed). Since the code was NOT already fixed and I fixed it, but an issue already exists... Hmm. The guidance: "File one issue per cause" and "don't file a problem that is already filed". The status "duplicate" with `duplicate: <issue#>` marks the crash group as duplicating an existing issue. But I did fix the code. 

I think the most sensible: for groups already filed, still fix the code, write reproducer, and mark status "duplicate" with the existing issue number? But then "fixed" status requires the issue I filed. Hmm, "duplicate" says "the open issue it duplicates". If I fixed it, marking duplicate loses the fix info... but the issue #101/#102 remain the tracking issues. The harness checks reproducer fail/pass for "fixed" status. For duplicate, "leave out [reproducer] for already_fixed and duplicate" — so no reproducer needed for duplicate.

